 voces guía de sus maquetas (","type":"text"},{"text":"demos","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), bromeaba autodenominándose \"La Voz de Mantequilla\".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bajo la firma de la disquera ","type":"text"},{"text":"Allegro Productions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (dirigida por Johnny Montaño, Manolo Ascona y Papo Daniel Hernández), lanzó un concepto festivo y jocoso que revolucionó el merengue de la época:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"«Dame del Pollito» (El Pollito):","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Tema inspirado en los ritmos de los batón ballet de su pueblo natal, que se convirtió en un fenómeno radial y de ventas masivo a nivel mundial. El impacto de la canción fue tal que la cadena británica BBC de Londres viajó a República Dominicana para realizarle un reportaje.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Otros Éxitos:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Popularizó temas de corte cómico y pegajoso como «El Maco» (","type":"text"},{"text":"A yo querer un maco...","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), «El Perro Chihuahua», «El Chivo», «La Pelota» y «La Chuleta».","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}'::jsonb, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = now() WHERE id = (SELECT d.id FROM editorial_documents d JOIN artists a ON a.id = d.owner_artist_id WHERE a.slug = 'freddy-gerardo' AND d.locale = 'es' AND d.document_type = 'artist_biography');
UPDATE artists SET bio_es = 'Freddy Gerardo Moreta nació en el Hospital Alejandro Cabral de San Juan de la Maguana, República Dominicana. Hijo de Fidelina Moreta (madre soltera de siete hijos, modista y dulcera) y Teófilo Peña Rosario (militar), creció en el popular barrio de Mesopotamia.

Su inclinación musical comenzó desde muy niño (a los 7 u 8 años) tras escuchar una agrupación típica en su barrio. Su primer acercamiento a la percusión lo hizo de forma empírica improvisando tamboras con galones de aceite y latas. Más adelante, vecinos como Seneo y Juanito le enseñaron formalmente la tambora y la güira.

Posteriormente aprendió a tocar la guitarra de forma autodidacta usando la de su vecino Teodoro Roa Lagares. Su talento innato fue advertido por el profesor Agustín Saldaña, quien convenció a su madre para inscribirlo en la Escuela de Bellas Artes y en la Academia Municipal de San Juan de la Maguana. Allí aprendió clarinete con el maestro Plinio Félix, piano clásico con la profesora Doña Molina y llegó a tocar el bajo y la lira en la banda de música municipal.

Durante su adolescencia formó junto a sus amigos la Agrupación Primavera. Con el tiempo, este grupo dio paso a Freddy Gerardo y su Orquesta, donde se desempeñaba como pianista, director musical y corista. Para ayudar económicamente en su hogar, trabajó desde joven como limpiabotas, dulcero y aprendiz de sastre.

**Trayectoria en Santo Domingo y Orquestas Populares**

A los 19 o 20 años, el músico sanjuanero "Psycho Colón" lo buscó en San Juan para incorporarlo como pianista en la discoteca del Hotel Napolitano en Santo Domingo. Una vez radicado en la capital, se integró activamente en la escena del merengue nacional:

- Richie Ricardo ("El Natural"): Ingresó como pianista y llegó a ser su director musical, aportando el característico estilo de piano influenciado por Andrés Mejías. Con esta orquesta realizó sus primeros viajes internacionales a Estados Unidos.  
- Sergio Hernández: Tras su salida del grupo de Richie Ricardo, perteneció a la orquesta de Sergio Hernández.  
- Carlos Manuel El Zafiro: Se integró como pianista y director musical durante la etapa final del artista antes de radicarse en Estados Unidos, realizando giras por Aruba, Curazao y Nueva York.  
- Rubby Pérez: En 1993 ingresó como pianista a la orquesta de Rubby Pérez por recomendación de los músicos Tati y Luis Rivera, permaneciendo durante unos cinco años. Durante este periodo se consolidó como un cotizado pianista dentro de los repertorios más exigentes del merengue dominicano.

**Faceta como Compositor y Arreglista**

Paralelamente a su labor como instrumentista, Freddy Gerardo desarrolló una prolífica carrera como compositor y arreglista para orquestas de renombre internacional:

- Los Sabrosos del Merengue (Puerto Rico): Compuso y arregló el tema «Colina» (el primer gran éxito grabado por Joseph Fonseca con la agrupación), además del clásico navideño «Pagando el 71».  
- Rikarena: Compuso y arregló el éxito «Antonio Pérez».  
- Jossie Esteban y La Patrulla 15 / La Banda 2000 / Rokabanda: Escribió y arregló varios temas, entre ellos «El viejo» y «El grillo».

**El Fenómeno Comercial y "La Voz de Mantequilla"**

A finales de la década de 1990, de manera accidental, Freddy Gerardo decidió emprender su propio proyecto musical como líder y cantante. Al grabar las voces guía de sus maquetas (demos), bromeaba autodenominándose "La Voz de Mantequilla".

Bajo la firma de la disquera Allegro Productions (dirigida por Johnny Montaño, Manolo Ascona y Papo Daniel Hernández), lanzó un concepto festivo y jocoso que revolucionó el merengue de la época:

- «Dame del Pollito» (El Pollito): Tema inspirado en los ritmos de los batón ballet de su pueblo natal, que se convirtió en un fenómeno radial y de ventas masivo a nivel mundial. El impacto de la canción fue tal que la cadena británica BBC de Londres viajó a República Dominicana para realizarle un reportaje.  
- Otros Éxitos: Popularizó temas de corte cómico y pegajoso como «El Maco» (A yo querer un maco...), «El Perro Chihuahua», «El Chivo», «La Pelota» y «La Chuleta».' WHERE slug = 'freddy-gerardo';

UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Marcos Yaroide Mejía Rambalde (Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic, October 7, 1975) is a Dominican singer, songwriter, and pastor, recognized as one of the most influential figures in contemporary Christian music in the Spanish-speaking world. His career spans more than two decades, beginning with his role as a member of the duo ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"ea351de4-218b-469d-a752-d1c46330d53d","displayText":"Tercer Cielo","occurrenceId":"68bcd503-f161-4e0b-a04e-87c73ba5a058"}},{"text":" and continuing through a strong solo career defined by spiritual pop aesthetics, testimonial lyrics, and active pastoral work.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Origins and Spiritual Formation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He is the son of Dominican accordionist Dionisio Mejía, known as “","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"e07267a0-7f9d-4ef5-928c-d4ade7dc921e","displayText":"Guandulito","occurrenceId":"05d2ecea-ded9-4c91-aaef-ecbf35d3bfd5"}},{"text":",” and grew up in a musical environment deeply connected to Dominican folk traditions. At age 12, he recounts having a spiritual experience that shaped his life: a dream in which he heard a trumpet and saw Christians ascending to heaven, while being told he had not yet “bought his ticket” and needed to do so “because there is still time.” This episode became a thematic foundation for much of his work, centered on salvation, hope, and spiritual life.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical Career","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His professional career began in 1999 when he joined Juan Carlos Rodríguez to form the duo Tercer Cielo. The project quickly became a major reference in Christian youth music, blending pop ballads with messages of faith. Their album Primer Amor sold more than 150,000 copies across Latin America and the United States, earning double gold certification and multiple recognitions in awards such as Tu Música and Paoli. Songs like «El rapto», «Ella y él», «Dios no te olvida», «Algún día», and «Ahora tengo más» became classics of contemporary Christian music. After three albums and nearly a decade of work, Yaroide ended his time with Tercer Cielo in 2006 to begin his solo career.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His solo debut came that same year with Cielos Abiertos, featuring the standout track «Todo se lo debo a Él». In 2008 he released Mi Mejor Alabanza, distributed by Machete Music, a label affiliated with Universal Music Latino. The version of «Todo se lo debo a Él» featuring urban artist Divino entered Billboard’s Latin Pop Songs chart, expanding his reach beyond the strictly Christian market. In 2010 he released Del Cielo a la Tierra, with songs such as «Mi trabajo es creer» and «Que se abran los cielos», solidifying his modern spiritual pop style.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2012 he released Todavía Hay Esperanza, which produced hits like «Bendice tu pueblo» and «Por una como ella», the latter remixed in merengue with Puerto Rican group Grupo Manía in 2013, demonstrating his ability to engage with tropical music while maintaining his Christian identity. In 2014 he released 15 Años Después Live, a live project commemorating his trajectory from Tercer Cielo to his solo work. His most recent studio album, La Vida Es (2017), explores themes of purpose, gratitude, and spiritual interpretation of everyday life.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical Style and Collaborations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Throughout his career he has collaborated with artists such as Divino, Alex Zurdo, ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"18e7fdfa-e697-440a-9402-b1b0fd01b952","displayText":"Redimi2","occurrenceId":"f6974ab6-d223-4711-b7e8-a0ffdeb7249d"}},{"text":", R.K.M & Ken-Y, Cultura Profética, Abraham Velásquez, and others, positioning himself as a bridge between Christian music and the broader Latin pop and urban scene. His style blends pop ballads, tropical elements, and lyrics focused on faith, restoration, inner struggle, and hope, using accessible language for both youth and adult audiences.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Personal Life and Ministry","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He is married to Laura Cardenes and is the father of Avril, Eden, Marko, and Aaron. Together they serve as pastors in the Dominican Republic, connected to the Centro de Adoración y Alabanza Cielos Abiertos, where they carry out community and ministerial work. Yaroide also participates occasionally in the media platform Alofoke Radio, where he discusses biblical topics and spiritual debates, extending his pastoral role into the media sphere.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Recognition and Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He has been nominated several times for the Premios Soberano in categories related to contemporary Christian music. His legacy is recognized both for his contribution to the expansion of Spanish-language Christian pop and for his influence within Dominican culture, where his figure connects the musical tradition inherited from “Guandulito” with the modernization of faith-based music in the country.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = now() WHERE id = (SELECT d.id FROM editorial_documents d JOIN artists a ON a.id = d.owner_artist_id WHERE a.slug = 'marcos-yaroide' AND d.locale = 'en' AND d.document_type = 'artist_biography');
UPDATE artists SET bio_en = 'Marcos Yaroide Mejía Rambalde (Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic, October 7, 1975) is a Dominican singer, songwriter, and pastor, recognized as one of the most influential figures in contemporary Christian music in the Spanish-speaking world. His career spans more than two decades, beginning with his role as a member of the duo Tercer Cielo and continuing through a strong solo career defined by spiritual pop aesthetics, testimonial lyrics, and active pastoral work.

**Origins and Spiritual Formation**

He is the son of Dominican accordionist Dionisio Mejía, known as “Guandulito,” and grew up in a musical environment deeply connected to Dominican folk traditions. At age 12, he recounts having a spiritual experience that shaped his life: a dream in which he heard a trumpet and saw Christians ascending to heaven, while being told he had not yet “bought his ticket” and needed to do so “because there is still time.” This episode became a thematic foundation for much of his work, centered on salvation, hope, and spiritual life.

**Musical Career**

His professional career began in 1999 when he joined Juan Carlos Rodríguez to form the duo Tercer Cielo. The project quickly became a major reference in Christian youth music, blending pop ballads with messages of faith. Their album Primer Amor sold more than 150,000 copies across Latin America and the United States, earning double gold certification and multiple recognitions in awards such as Tu Música and Paoli. Songs like «El rapto», «Ella y él», «Dios no te olvida», «Algún día», and «Ahora tengo más» became classics of contemporary Christian music. After three albums and nearly a decade of work, Yaroide ended his time with Tercer Cielo in 2006 to begin his solo career.

His solo debut came that same year with Cielos Abiertos, featuring the standout track «Todo se lo debo a Él». In 2008 he released Mi Mejor Alabanza, distributed by Machete Music, a label affiliated with Universal Music Latino. The version of «Todo se lo debo a Él» featuring urban artist Divino entered Billboard’s Latin Pop Songs chart, expanding his reach beyond the strictly Christian market. In 2010 he released Del Cielo a la Tierra, with songs such as «Mi trabajo es creer» and «Que se abran los cielos», solidifying his modern spiritual pop style.

In 2012 he released Todavía Hay Esperanza, which produced hits like «Bendice tu pueblo» and «Por una como ella», the latter remixed in merengue with Puerto Rican group Grupo Manía in 2013, demonstrating his ability to engage with tropical music while maintaining his Christian identity. In 2014 he released 15 Años Después Live, a live project commemorating his trajectory from Tercer Cielo to his solo work. His most recent studio album, La Vida Es (2017), explores themes of purpose, gratitude, and spiritual interpretation of everyday life.

**Musical Style and Collaborations**

Throughout his career he has collaborated with artists such as Divino, Alex Zurdo, Redimi2, R.K.M & Ken-Y, Cultura Profética, Abraham Velásquez, and others, positioning himself as a bridge between Christian music and the broader Latin pop and urban scene. His style blends pop ballads, tropical elements, and lyrics focused on faith, restoration, inner struggle, and hope, using accessible language for both youth and adult audiences.

**Personal Life and Ministry**

He is married to Laura Cardenes and is the father of Avril, Eden, Marko, and Aaron. Together they serve as pastors in the Dominican Republic, connected to the Centro de Adoración y Alabanza Cielos Abiertos, where they carry out community and ministerial work. Yaroide also participates occasionally in the media platform Alofoke Radio, where he discusses biblical topics and spiritual debates, extending his pastoral role into the media sphere.

**Recognition and Legacy**

He has been nominated several times for the Premios Soberano in categories related to contemporary Christian music. His legacy is recognized both for his contribution to the expansion of Spanish-language Christian pop and for his influence within Dominican culture, where his figure connects the musical tradition inherited from “Guandulito” with the modernization of faith-based music in the country.' WHERE slug = 'marcos-yaroide';

